problems.problem:
On the sides of a triangle $ABC$, triangles $AEB$ and $AFC$ are constructed externally such that $\angle AEB=2 \alpha$, $\angle AFC= 2 \beta$, $AE=EB$ and $AF=FC$. On $BC$, triangle $BDC$ is constructed externally with $\angle DBC= \beta$ and $\angle BCD= \alpha$. Prove that:

1. $DA$ is perpendicular to $EF$;

2. if $T$ is the projection of $D$ on $BC$, then $\frac{DA}{EF}= 2 \frac{DT}{BC}$.

Prove that there exists $n_{0} \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for every $m \geq n_{0}$ there exist three positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$ such that

(i) $m^3 < a < b < c < (m+1)^3$;

(ii) $abc$ is the cube of an integer.

There are $10001$ students at a university. Some students join together to form several clubs (a student may belong to different clubs), and some clubs join together to form several societies (a club may belong to different societies). There are $k$ societies in total. Suppose that the following conditions hold:

i) each pair of students are in exactly one common club;

ii) for each student and each society, the student is in exactly one club of the society;

iii) each club has an odd number of students, and a club with $2m+1$ students ($m$ a positive integer) is in exactly $m$ societies.

Find all possible values of $k$.

Let $ABC$ be a triangle and let $P$ be a point in the plane of $ABC$ that lies inside the angle $BAC$ but outside triangle $ABC$.

(a) Prove that any two of the following statements imply the third:

(i) the circumcentre of triangle $PBC$ lies on the ray $PA$;

(ii) the circumcentre of triangle $CPA$ lies on the ray $PB$;

(iii) the circumcentre of triangle $APB$ lies on the ray $PC$.

(b) Prove that if the conditions in (a) hold, then the circumcentres of triangles $BPC$, $CPA$ and $APB$ lie on the circumcircle of triangle $ABC$.

Let $A_1,A_2,\ldots,A_n$ be subsets of a finite set $S$ with $|A_j|=8$ for each $j$. For a subset $B$ of $S$ let $F(B)=\{j \mid 1\le j\le n,\ A_j \subseteq B\}$. Suppose that for each subset $B$ of $S$ at least one of the following conditions holds:

(a) $|B| > 25$;

(b) $F(B)=\varnothing$;

(c) $\bigcap_{j\in F(B)} A_j \neq \varnothing$.

Prove that $A_1\cap A_2 \cap \cdots \cap A_n \neq \varnothing$.

Let $ABC$ be a triangle each of whose angles is greater than $30^{\circ}$, with sides $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$ and area $S$. A circle with centre $P$ cuts the segment $BC$ at $T,Q$, the segment $CA$ at $K,L$ and the segment $AB$ at $M,N$, these points lying on the circle in counterclockwise order. Suppose further that the triangles $PQK$, $PLM$, $PNT$ are equilateral. Prove that:

a) the radius of the circle is $\frac{2abc}{a^2+b^2+c^2+4\sqrt{3}S}$;

b) $a\cdot AP=b\cdot BP=c\cdot CP$.

Find all positive integers $n$ satisfying the conditions

a) $n^2=(a+1)^3-a^3$ for some integer $a$;

b) $2n+119$ is a perfect square.

Let $T$ be a non-empty finite set of positive integers. A subset $S$ of $T$ is called good if for every $t\in T$ there exists $s\in S$ with $\gcd(t,s) >1$. Let $$A=\{(X,Y)\mid X\subseteq T,\ Y\subseteq T,\ \gcd(x,y)=1 \text{ for all } x\in X,\ y\in Y\}.$$ Prove that:

a) if $X_0$ is not good, then the number of pairs $(X_0,Y)$ in $A$ is even;

b) the number of good subsets of $T$ is odd.

For $j=1,2,3$ let $x_{j},y_{j}$ be non-zero real numbers, and let $v_{j}=x_{j}+y_{j}$. Suppose that the following statements hold:

• $x_{1}x_{2}x_{3}=-y_{1}y_{2}y_{3}$;

• $x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}+x_{3}^{2}=y_{1}^{2}+y_{2}^{2}+y_{3}^2$;

• $v_{1},v_{2},v_{3}$ satisfy the triangle inequality;

• $v_{1}^{2},v_{2}^{2},v_{3}^{2}$ also satisfy the triangle inequality.

Prove that exactly one of $x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},y_{1},y_{2},y_{3}$ is negative.

Let $\mathbb N$ denote the set of all positive integers. Show that there exist two nonempty subsets $A$ and $B$ of $\mathbb N$ such that

1. $A\cap B=\{1\}$;

2. every number in $\mathbb N$ can be expressed as the product of a number in $A$ and a number in $B$;

3. each prime number is a divisor of some number in $A$ and also of some number in $B$;

4. one of the sets $A$ and $B$ has the following property: if its elements are written as $x_1<x_2<x_3<\cdots$, then for any given positive integer $M$ there exists $k\in \mathbb N$ such that $x_{k+1}-x_k\ge M$;

5. each set contains infinitely many composite numbers.

For each $n \ge 2$ define the polynomial $$f_n(x)=x^n-x^{n-1}-\cdots-x-1.$$ Prove that

(a) for each $n \ge 2$, the equation $f_n(x)=0$ has a unique positive real root $\alpha_n$;

(b) $(\alpha_n)$ is a strictly increasing sequence;

(c) $\lim_{n \to \infty} \alpha_n=2$.

Given any set $S$ of positive integers, show that at least one of the following two assertions holds:

(1) there exist distinct finite subsets $F$ and $G$ of $S$ such that $\sum_{x\in F}1/x=\sum_{x\in G}1/x$;

(2) there exists a positive rational number $r<1$ such that $\sum_{x\in F}1/x\neq r$ for all finite subsets $F$ of $S$.

In a school, every pair of students are either friends or strangers. Friendship is mutual, and no student is a friend of himself. A sequence of (not necessarily distinct) students $A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_{2023}$ is called mischievous if

• the total number of friends of $A_1$ is odd;

• $A_i$ and $A_{i+1}$ are friends for $i=1, 2, \ldots, 2022$;

• the total number of friends of $A_{2023}$ is even.

Prove that the total number of mischievous sequences is even.
